course: dictionary
lesson: 5384
title: Knobbly
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Knobbly means covered with small round lumps or bumps. Knob is a round lump, and the -ly ending makes it describe something. «Mountain bikes have wide, knobbly tyres for grip on mud.» The bumps on the tyres grip the mud, and smooth tyres would slip more.
explain: Knobbly is an adjective, so it describes a noun. We can compare things with knobblier and knobbliest: a root with many bumps is knobblier than a smooth one, and the knobbliest root is the most bumpy of all.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «They came from the cold Andes mountains of South America, where thousands of purple, red and knobbly kinds still grow.» The kinds are described as bumpy, and the sense of the word tells you they are potatoes. Ask yourself what would fit: a word like lumpy fits, so knobbly does too.
fact: Knobbly means covered with small round lumps or bumps.
fact: «Knobbly knees» are bony knees that stick out.
fact: Knobbly can describe old trees, tree roots, potatoes, walking sticks and old hands.
fact: Knobblier is the comparative form, and knobbliest is the superlative form.
fact: In American English the spelling is «knobby».
exercise: «Mountain bikes have wide, knobbly tyres for grip on mud.» What does knobbly mean here? | *Covered with small lumps that grip the mud | Very smooth and shiny | Bright red in colour | Very thin and light
exercise: Which word means the same as knobbly? | flat | smooth | shiny | *lumpy
exercise: «Knobbly knees» are knees that do what? | *Are bony and stick out | Shake when you are scared | Are smooth and round | Are covered in mud
test: Which word is the comparative form of knobbly? | knobbliest | *knobblier | knobbles | knobbled
test: «They came from the cold Andes mountains of South America, where thousands of purple, red and knobbly kinds still grow.» What does knobbly mean in this context? | Very cold | Growing very tall | Smooth and shiny | *Covered with lumps or bumps
exam: Which sentence uses knobbly correctly? | The knobbly sky was bright blue | The lake was knobbly and still | *The old walking stick was knobbly, covered in lumps | Knobbly is a verb that means to run fast
